                <pb facs="0366" n="360" sameAs="frus:frus1901China/pg360"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Conger to Mr.
                            Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1901-02-06"><hi rendition="#italic">February 6,
                        1901</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. Conger reports that a conference was held on the 5th instant by the foreign
                    ministers with the Chinese plenipotentiaries, who presented the difficulties in
                    the way of executing Prince Tuan, Duke Lan, and Tung Fu-hsiang. They promise
                    death of Chuang and Yü Hsien, but urge leniency for the others, and beg that
                    they and the court be not placed in too difficult a position. Subsequently the
                    foreign ministers agreed to demand capital sentence, to be immediately commuted
                    to exile, for Tuan and Lan, and death penalty for the others mentioned in the
                    decree, adding Yü Hsien, Chi Hsiu, and Hsü Cheng-yu, the two latter being now
                    prisoners of the Japanese in Peking. Posthumous honors for the members of the
                    Tsungli Yamen, executed last summer, are also to be demanded.)</p>
            </div>
        </body>
